<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="runner-fleet"
      updated="August 23, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"groups-labels", "Groups and labels"},
        {"enrollment-keys", "Enrollment keys"},
        {"pack-credentials", "Giving packs their credentials"},
        {"connectivity", "Online, offline, and stuck runs"},
        {"updating", "Updating"},
        {"toolbox", "The host-side toolbox"},
        {"removing", "Removing a runner"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="runner-fleet">
        <:dek>
          The
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >quickstart</.link>
          gets one runner online. This page covers everything after: groups and labels,
          enrollment keys, pack credentials, connectivity, updates, and removal.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/runner-fleet.webp"
        title="emisar console · Runners"
        caption="The fleet by group — each runner's version, hostname, last heartbeat, and connection status."
        alt="The Runners page: the fleet grouped by runner group — each runner's name, agent version, hostname, last heartbeat, and connection status. Three runners show a green connected badge; one shows an amber offline badge with when it was last seen."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="groups-labels">Groups and labels</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every runner declares one group string and free-form labels in <.docs_inline_code>/etc/emisar/config.yaml</.docs_inline_code>.
        Group examples include
        <.docs_inline_code>cassandra-prod</.docs_inline_code>
        and <.docs_inline_code>web</.docs_inline_code>. Label examples include
        <.docs_inline_code>role=db</.docs_inline_code>
        and <.docs_inline_code>region=us-east-1</.docs_inline_code>. Groups do real
        work: runbook steps target them, per-member runner scopes follow them, and an agent can
        address every runner in one group at once. Name a group after a fleet tier. The installer
        defaults the group to the hostname, which works for one host — change it when you add a
        second.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Install packs generously. Give each group every pack that matches what runs there — the
        <.docs_inline_code>postgres</.docs_inline_code>
        pack on the database tier,
        <.docs_inline_code>nginx</.docs_inline_code>
        on the web tier — and the observation packs everywhere. The more actions an agent can
        reach, the more it can do for you, and breadth is safe: policy decides what actually
        runs, so reads run at once while risky work waits for a person.
        <.docs_inline_code>sudo emisar pack suggest</.docs_inline_code>
        lists the published packs that match a host.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="enrollment-keys">Enrollment keys</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Two key models.</strong>
            An
            <.docs_inline_code>emkey-enroll-…</.docs_inline_code>
            key is <strong class="text-zinc-200">single-use</strong>
            by default: its first registration spends it, and concurrent attempts with the same
            key cannot both succeed — right for connecting one host. Mark it
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">reusable</strong>
            to keep enrolling hosts until it expires or reaches its maximum use count (blank for
            unlimited) — right for launch templates and fleet provisioning.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Shown once, exchanged for a token.</strong>
            The raw key appears once, at creation. On first connect the runner exchanges it for
            its own
            <.docs_inline_code>rnrtok-…</.docs_inline_code>
            token, which handles every reconnect from then on.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/runner-credentials"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Runner credentials</.link>
            covers the token's lifetime, refresh, and recovery.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Mint at install time.</strong>
            <a
              href={~p"/app/runners/install"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Runners → Connect a runner</a>
            generates the install one-liner with a
            fresh single-use key filled in. For fleet provisioning (cloud-init, Packer, Ansible),
            mint one reusable key under
            <a
              href={~p"/app/runners/keys"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Runners → Enrollment keys</a>
            and inject it as <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY</.docs_inline_code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Revoke from the console.</strong>
            Revoking a key blocks new registrations; hosts already enrolled keep running on their
            own tokens. To take a runner itself offline — disable (reversible) or delete
            (terminal) — see <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/runner-credentials"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Runner credentials</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To change the key a host carries, reinstall with the new
        <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY</.docs_inline_code>
        — the runner re-registers under its
        <.docs_inline_code>runner.id</.docs_inline_code>
        or current hostname;
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-credentials"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Runner credentials</.link>
        has the step-by-step. A replacement host with a new hostname simply enrolls as a new
        runner.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="pack-credentials">Giving packs their credentials</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Packs that talk to a service — Nomad, Consul, Postgres — read credentials from the
        runner's <em>environment</em>, never from call arguments, and the control plane does not
        distribute or store them. An action can still print a value, and if redaction misses it,
        it can appear in run output. Two steps on the host:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="config"><span class="text-zinc-500"># 1. /etc/emisar/runner.env (mode 0600) — the values</span>
NOMAD_ADDR=http://127.0.0.1:4646
NOMAD_TOKEN=&lt;acl-token&gt;

<span class="text-zinc-500"># 2. /etc/emisar/config.yaml — allowlist the names</span>
execution:
  inherit_env:
    - NOMAD_ADDR
    - NOMAD_TOKEN</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Only allowlisted names reach an action's process, in addition to the
        <.docs_inline_code>PATH</.docs_inline_code>
        and locale baseline. The runner's own environment and token never reach it. Restart the
        service after editing. Run
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar pack info &lt;id&gt;</.docs_inline_code>
        to see what each pack needs, and
        <.docs_inline_code>sudo emisar pack verify</.docs_inline_code>
        to make sure the wiring works — it probes every installed pack's verify action and prints ok,
        failed, or skipped per pack.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="connectivity">Online, offline, and stuck runs</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner sends a heartbeat every 30 seconds, and the console's
        <.status_badge status={:online} />
        badge reflects it. Either side closes a connection that goes quiet; the runner reconnects on its own
        with backoff and advertises its catalog again. In-flight actions keep running through
        the gap, and their results arrive on the new connection. If a runner stays offline past
        a two-minute grace period, its in-flight runs become errored with an explanation —
        nothing stays "running" forever.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="updating">Updating</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># runner binary — re-run the installer; configs and packs are preserved</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash

<span class="text-zinc-500"># packs — update from the registry; the update reloads the runner (no restart, no dropped runs)</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack update linux-core</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Reload (SIGHUP) re-reads packs and re-advertises the catalog on the live connection. An
        unrecognized hash — your own pack, or an edited one — waits for trust on the Packs
        page.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To roll the binary across a fleet with canaries, batches, and rollback, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-upgrades"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Upgrade runners</.link>. To do the same for packs, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Roll out and roll back packs</.link>. A binary upgrade never touches packs, and a
        pack update never touches the binary.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="toolbox">The host-side toolbox</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> journalctl -u emisar -f          <span class="text-zinc-500"># service logs</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar action list           <span class="text-zinc-500"># what this host advertises</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack list             <span class="text-zinc-500"># loaded packs + hashes</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar events tail -f        <span class="text-zinc-500"># the local JSONL journal, live</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar audit verify --all    <span class="text-zinc-500"># verify the journal's hash chain</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The CLI defaults to <.docs_inline_code>--config /etc/emisar/config.yaml</.docs_inline_code>,
        so these commands work on an installed host. The runner runs as its own unprivileged
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar</.docs_inline_code>
        user; granting an action root — a scoped polkit rule, or running the service as root —
        is covered on the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/host-install" <> "#user"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Linux host page</.link>, and the
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security model</.link>
        covers the rest of the host protections.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="removing">Removing a runner</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># remove binary + service + cached token</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash -s -- --uninstall
<span class="text-zinc-500"># …or purge everything, including the local journal</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash -s -- --uninstall --purge</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Then delete the runner from <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>. Its run history and audit events stay.
        <.docs_inline_code>--purge</.docs_inline_code>
        also deletes the local journal, configuration, and
        <.docs_inline_code>runner.env</.docs_inline_code>
        secrets. Export the journal first if you want to keep it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        That is the routine retirement. For a host you no longer trust, follow
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-incidents" <> "#runner-host"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security incidents</.link>
        instead — evidence first, then containment.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>

      <.docs_h3 id="never-appears">It never appears in the fleet</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The installer finished, but the runner is not in the Runners list at all. It never
        completed its first registration, so there is no identity to show. Read the host log
        first — the register line carries the status that stopped it:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo journalctl -u emisar -n 200</.docs_code>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[720px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">The log shows</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What it means</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What to do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                <.docs_inline_code>401</.docs_inline_code>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                The enrollment key was spent, expired, out of uses, or revoked.
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Mint a fresh key and reinstall with it.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                <.docs_inline_code>400 invalid_external_id</.docs_inline_code>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                The host offered no usable identity — the runner uses its configured <.docs_inline_code>runner.id</.docs_inline_code>, or its
                hostname when none is set.
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Set a valid
                <.docs_inline_code>runner.id</.docs_inline_code>
                — at least one nonblank character, at most 255 — and restart the service.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                <.docs_inline_code>409 runner_name_taken</.docs_inline_code>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Another runner in this account already holds this name, online or not — a rebuilt
                host, a restored image, or two hosts sharing one hostname.
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Delete the existing runner in
                <a
                  href={~p"/app/runners"}
                  class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
                >Runners</a>
                to free the name, or set
                <.docs_inline_code>runner.id</.docs_inline_code>
                in this host's config.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                <.docs_inline_code>402 runner_limit_exceeded</.docs_inline_code>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                The account is at its plan's runner cap — the response shows the plan and the
                limit.
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Delete a runner the account no longer uses, or move to a plan with more —
                <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/billing"}
                  class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
                >Billing and plans</.link>
                has the numbers.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On the name conflict: a runner's name is unique in the account — by default it is the
        hostname of the host it runs on, and it cannot be renamed after the runner is created.
        Set
        <.docs_inline_code>runner.id</.docs_inline_code>
        in the config (or
        <.docs_inline_code>RUNNER_ID</.docs_inline_code>
        at install) to register under a declared name and identity instead of the hostname.
        A deleted runner cannot be restored — the host enrolls again as a new one, and
        <a href="#removing" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Removing a runner</a>
        covers the difference from disabling.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The log shows no failure — the connect hangs.</strong>
            A hang is a blocked path, not a bad credential. The runner needs outbound TLS to
            your emisar origin and nothing inbound — the exact hosts and ports are in <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/network-requirements"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Network requirements</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Contact support</strong>
            when a freshly minted, unused key still returns <.docs_inline_code>401</.docs_inline_code>, or registration
            returns a status the log cannot explain. For enrollment keys and their caps, see
            <a href="#enrollment-keys" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Enrollment keys</a>
            above.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h3 id="offline">It is offline or keeps reconnecting</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner registered once and is now offline. Reconnecting is normal: the runner
        retries on its own, advertises its packs and actions again when it connects, and an
        action already running continues through the gap.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">First, look at the host.</strong>
            <.docs_inline_code>systemctl status emisar</.docs_inline_code>
            shows whether the process is running.
            <.docs_inline_code>sudo emisar doctor</.docs_inline_code>
            checks config, credentials, packs, action binaries, service state, and
            reachability, all without a session to the control plane.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Then check the identity, not the host.</strong>
            A disabled runner gets a
            <.docs_inline_code>403</.docs_inline_code>
            and retries with its existing token, so enabling it brings the host back without
            shell access — a disabled account behaves the same way for every runner in it. A
            deleted runner cannot be restored: it gets a <.docs_inline_code>401</.docs_inline_code>, discards its cached
            token, stops retrying, and that host must enroll again.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Contact support</strong>
            when the service is up, the runner is enabled,
            <.docs_inline_code>doctor</.docs_inline_code>
            passes including reachability, and the fleet still shows it offline after several
            reconnect attempts.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h3 id="crash-loop">The service failed or is crash-looping</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        systemd reports <.docs_inline_code>failed</.docs_inline_code>. After five starts in
        five minutes the service stops retrying on purpose, so a revoked credential cannot hammer
        the control plane. Fix what the log shows, then re-arm the service — starting without
        resetting the failure counter does nothing:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo systemctl reset-failed emisar &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl start emisar</.docs_code>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Then check for a second process.</strong>
            A runner locks its data directory at boot — only one process can own the dispatch
            and replay state. A stray manual
            <.docs_inline_code>emisar connect</.docs_inline_code>
            beside the service is the usual cause.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Contact support</strong>
            when
            <.docs_inline_code>doctor</.docs_inline_code>
            reports every check green and the service still exits. For the install layout, the
            service unit, and the paths it writes, see <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Install on a host</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
